      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1938, several Nunamiut families returned to the Brooks Range, eventually establishing the settlement that became Anaktuvuk Pass.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anaktuvuk_Pass%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The trek from Sulupaat to Tulugak Lake and onward to Anaktuvuk Pass earned the name 'Long Walk' among local oral historians.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anaktuvuk_Pass%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Anaktuvuk Pass lies in the heart of Gates of the Arctic National Park and Preserve, surrounded by the Brooks Range.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anaktuvuk_Pass%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Anaktuvuk Pass remains the sole inland Iñupiat settlement, home to the Nunamiut people who hunt caribou.",
